package wk:clipboard;

/// The HOST's system clipboard, granted by wiring a node to a Clipboard node
/// on the canvas. A wasm node can never reach NSPasteboard or an X11 selection
/// itself; wk's local client owns the one `arboard::Clipboard` and publishes
/// what it holds, exactly the way a Screen Capture node publishes frames. So
/// clipboard access is an explicit, visible, revocable capability — not an
/// ambient one — and that matters more here than it does for capture: whatever
/// the user last copied ANYWHERE on the machine (a password manager, a
/// terminal, a bank site) is readable through `get`, and `set` lets a node
/// plant content for the user to paste somewhere else. This is a genuine
/// cross-sandbox side channel and it is default-deny.
///
/// GATING, in two independent halves. A node is allowed nothing until it is
/// wired to a Clipboard node, and even then `read` and `write` are separate
/// capability-token actions, so a token can grant copy-out without paste-in:
///
///     wk token attenuate <node> \
///       'check if operation($k, $t, $a), $k != "clipboard" || $a == "write"'
///
/// leaves `set` working and makes `get` return `none` forever. The base rule
/// `can_use($k,$t,$a) <- wired($k,$t), operation($k,$t,$a)` is what makes the
/// unwired case deny; `clipboard` deliberately has NO no-wire exception of the
/// sort `scene` and `exec` have, because those grant no cross-sandbox
/// authority and this does.
interface clipboard {
    /// What the host clipboard holds, plus a sequence number.
    ///
    /// `seq` starts at 1 for the first text the host ever observes and
    /// increments only when the text actually CHANGES. It exists because
    /// `arboard` has no change notification of any kind — no `changeCount`,
    /// no watcher — so the host produces this by polling and diffing, and a
    /// guest that wants "did someone else copy something?" compares seq rather
    /// than the string. It is also how a toolkit tells "the clipboard is still
    /// mine" from "another app took it": remember the seq your own `set`
    /// produced and compare (this is what QWkClipboard::ownsMode does).
    record snapshot {
        seq: u64,
        text: string,
    }

    /// The host clipboard's current contents.
    ///
    /// `none` means the node may not read it — no Clipboard wire, or a token
    /// that denies `clipboard`/`read` — OR the host has no clipboard at all
    /// (a headless machine with no display server), OR it holds something
    /// that is not text. Those cases are deliberately indistinguishable to the
    /// guest: a denied node must not be able to probe for the existence of a
    /// clipboard it cannot see. The host logs the first denied read per node,
    /// so the deny is visible in `wk logs` to the person running wk, just not
    /// to the sandbox.
    ///
    /// Cheap to call: it reads a value the host has already published, so a
    /// toolkit may call it from inside a synchronous `QPlatformClipboard::
    /// mimeData()` without blocking on the window system.
    get: func() -> option<snapshot>;

    /// Put `text` on the host clipboard.
    ///
    /// Returns nothing and a denied write is silently dropped, like
    /// `wk:midi`'s `send` — a node that may not write learns nothing from
    /// trying. The write is queued for the client's next event-loop pass
    /// rather than applied inline, because the only thread that may touch
    /// `arboard` is the one that owns the window system connection.
    ///
    /// TEXT ONLY, and that is a deliberate cut rather than a stub. `arboard`'s
    /// read side is text or an RGBA image and there is no `get_html`, so a
    /// `list<tuple<string, list<u8>>>` MIME map would be a two-entry map
    /// pretending to be general. A toolkit loses nothing in-process: Qt's
    /// QWkClipboard keeps the QMimeData the app itself set and hands it back
    /// verbatim while the app still owns the clipboard, so copying an image
    /// inside one node and pasting it back is lossless; only the hop across
    /// the host is text. Images can be added later as a separate
    /// `get-image`/`set-image` pair — `arboard`'s `image-data` feature is
    /// already on, and its RGBA8+w+h shape is byte-for-byte `wk:capture`'s
    /// `frame` — without retrofitting a MIME map onto this.
    set: func(text: string);
}

/// The host side: wk implements (imports) the clipboard interface for guests.
world clipboard-host {
    import clipboard;
}
